- How would you optimize the inner max to O(n) using a deque when k is large?
- How would you reconstruct the actual partition that yields the maximum sum?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine arrays/linked lists (deques), hash maps, and a heap to maintain a sliding-window top-K efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window, expiration, lazy deletions in a heap
- Per-user state via linked-list/deque and running sums
- Time/space trade-offs for real-time top-K

## Code Example
```javascript
class WaveTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.userMap = new Map(); // user_id -> {queue: [], sum}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // stores [sum, user_id]
  }
  addEvent(userId, score, ts) {
    let entry = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!entry) {
      entry = { queue: [], sum: 0 };
      this.userMap.set(userId, entry);
    }
    entry.queue.push([ts, score]);
    entry.sum += score;
    this.heap.push([entry.sum, userId]);
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const seen = new Map();
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [sum, uid] = this.heap.pop();
      const ent = this.userMap.get(uid);
      if (!ent) continue;
      // expire check
      this.expireUser(uid, currentTime);
      if (ent.sum !== sum) {
        this.heap.push([ent.sum, uid]);
        continue;
      }
      res.push(uid);
    }
    // Reinsert popped elements
    for (const uid of res) this.heap.push([this.userMap.get(uid).sum, uid]);
    return res;
  }
  expireUser(userId, currentTime) {
    const ent = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!ent) return;
    while (ent.queue.length && ent.queue[0][0] <= currentTime - this.W) {
      const [, val] = ent.queue.shift();
      ent.sum -= val;
    }
  }
}
```

